DATASET: BHARATTHERMAL-1, RELEASED AS A COLLECTIVE DATABASE

The dataset is held for a research publication in preparation and is not yet
distributed. When it is, it will be released in three parts under two licences,
joined on the detection identifier.

  Part A, thermal record and features         CC BY 4.0
    Detection identifier, location, time, brightness temperatures, radiative
    power, scan and track, sensor, confidence, the cyclic time encodings, and the
    five recurrence features computed from the detection record itself.

    This is the entire model feature set. All eighteen columns the baselines fit
    on are derived from NASA FIRMS and from the detection record's own history.
    None is derived from OpenStreetMap.

  Part B, land cover and asset context        CC BY 4.0
    Land cover class at the detection, from ESA WorldCover, and the distance to
    the nearest Global Energy Monitor asset valid on the detection date. Both
    upstream sources are CC BY 4.0 and require attribution only.

  Part C, OpenStreetMap context               ODbL 1.0
    Detection identifier, the state name, the distance to the nearest mapped
    industrial feature, and the weak label. Three columns.

    The weak label sits here because it is computed from the industrial distance
    among other inputs, so it is downstream of OpenStreetMap. Placing it on the
    permissive side would be the loosest reading available rather than the
    defensible one.

  Not distributed
    The distance to the nearest catalogued gas flare, from the EOG catalogue,
    which permits attribution but does not confirm redistribution.

The reason for the split is ODbL section 4.5, which provides that incorporating a
database into a Collective Database does not require the collection to be licensed
under ODbL, while the incorporated database remains under it. A Collective Database
is defined as a collection of independent databases that are databases in
themselves.

That definition is the condition, and it drives the structure. Part C is a separate
file with its own licence and its own attribution, joinable on the detection
identifier but not merged into the others. Shipped as one table the parts would
form a single Derivative Database under section 4.4, and share alike would apply to
the whole of it.

A practical consequence worth stating plainly. Part A alone is the complete feature
set and carries no share alike, so a reader can fit every baseline in this project
under CC BY 4.0. The labels are in Part C, so supervised use of the published
labels inherits ODbL. Anyone labelling the detections by another route uses Part A
freely.

This structure has not been confirmed with qualified advice and is stated as the
intended release rather than as a settled position. Whether these parts are
independent databases in themselves, or one database presented in several files, is
the question that decides it.

Separately, the EOG gas flare catalogue used during construction permits
attribution but does not confirm redistribution. Values derived from it are
published only as coarse distance bands, because exact distances from many known
points can be trilaterated back to the catalogue.


UPSTREAM SOURCES AND THEIR TERMS

  NASA FIRMS                     active fire detections, free and open,
                                 attribution requested
  OpenStreetMap                  ODbL 1.0, attribution and share alike on a
                                 derivative database
  Global Energy Monitor          CC BY 4.0
  ESA WorldCover                 CC BY 4.0
  Copernicus Sentinel-2          free with attribution. Contains modified
                                 Copernicus Sentinel data 2024
  EOG, Colorado School of Mines  attribution required, redistribution not
                                 confirmed

Full attribution text is in README.md.


